#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082904 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083356 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b2b6ea7-ea1c-5d6b-b786-bb0880c6af4a" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Inverse Function From a Mapping Diagram"}] :question/id #uuid "bca42c78-0735-587e-914b-7c9892f47d51" :question/math-academy-id "e-10986" :question/problem "![](images/0f/0fc4d1e69587fe42eeae88a5382638fa9cf6c7c1f83c6c82a607401ab0de4cec.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is represented by the mapping diagram above. What is $f^{-1}(-1)$?" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nThe function $f^{-1}(x)$ reverses the action of $f(x)$, as depicted below.\n\n![](images/47/47fb60dabc7ddfde9f5e6b1ee5e771b90263cda592b28e80c475bda482bcaf87.png)\n\nFrom the above, we see that\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(-1) = 4\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFrom the given mapping diagram, we have\n\n$$\nf(4) = -1\n$$\n\nApplying $f^{-1}$ to both sides of the above equation, and using the fact that\n\n$$\nf^{-1}\\left(f(x)\\right) = x\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{-1}(f(4)) &= f^{-1}(-1) \\\\\n4 &= f^{-1}(-1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(-1) = 4\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082905 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083357 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd1735b2-8684-5312-8315-ec6acb2daa21" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Inverse Function Given Function Data"}] :question/id #uuid "841640c2-1f50-5a13-b12b-bfe202e7621c" :question/math-academy-id "e-10987" :question/problem "| **$x$** | $-8$ | $-4$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $5$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $7$ | $3$ | $0$ | $-2$ | $-3$ | $-4$ | $-6$ |\n\nGiven the function $f(x)$ defined by the table above, what is the value of $f^{-1}(3)$?" :question/worked-solution "From the table, we have\n\n$$\nf(-4) = 3\n$$\n\nApplying $f^{-1}$ to both sides of the above equation, and using the fact that\n\n$$\nf^{-1}\\left(f(x)\\right) = x\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{-1}(f(-4)) &= f^{-1}(3) \\\\\n- 4 &= f^{-1}(3)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(3) = -4\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082906 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083358 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab89a7ad-8b73-5dde-b5ed-efac2deb67e1" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Inverse Function Using a Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "200ba5c0-9ec6-56e9-8ce4-24cee169ac8c" :question/math-academy-id "e-10988" :question/problem "![](images/e7/e714ae145a8d9f45dd2139f28866e6919fcf505d547352f0e615947583360723.png)\n\nThe graph of $y = f(x)$ is given above. The points $A,B,C$, and $D$ lie on the graph, as shown. What is the value of $f^{-1}(-3)$?" :question/worked-solution "The point $D$ on the graph has coordinates $(2,-3)$. Therefore,\n\n$$\nf(2) = -3\n$$\n\nApplying $f^{-1}$ to both sides of the above equation, and using the fact that\n\n$$\nf^{-1}\\left(f(x)\\right) = x\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{-1}(f(2)) &= f^{-1}(-3) \\\\\n2 &= f^{-1}(-3)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(-3) = 2\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082907 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083359 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "06e51d62-ad2a-51c3-b276-eeb60022ab48" :knowledge-point/title "Computing an Inverse Function Using the Definition"}] :question/id #uuid "062748b1-c85a-5c2c-8d1f-f1006e73a880" :question/math-academy-id "e-10989" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)=\\dfrac{x}{4}-2$ and $g(x)=4x-2k$. If $g$ is the inverse of $f$, then what is the value of $k$?" :question/worked-solution "If $g$ is the inverse of $f$, then we must have\n\n$$\ng(f(x)) = x\n$$\n\nTo find $g(f(x))$, we write down the function $g$ and replace every occurrence of $x$ with $f(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(x) &= 4x - 2k \\\\\ng(f(x)) &= 4f(x) - 2k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}f(x)} = {\\color{blue}\\dfrac{x}{4}-2}\n$$\n\ninto the right-hand side of our expression and simplify, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(f(x)) &= 4f(x) - 2k \\\\\n&= 4(\\frac{x}{4} - 2) - 2k \\\\\n&= x - 8 - 2k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause $g$ is the inverse of $f$, we also know that\n\n$$\ng(f(x)) = x\n$$\n\nEquating the above expression with $x$ and solving for $k$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx - 8 - 2k &= x \\\\\nx - 8 - 2k &= x \\\\\n- 8 - 2k &= 0 \\\\\n2k &= -8 \\\\\nk &= -4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083662 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083359 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "06e51d62-ad2a-51c3-b276-eeb60022ab48" :knowledge-point/title "Computing an Inverse Function Using the Definition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e4dfb789-7b74-5226-90a3-c10e49daa132" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080904} {:answer/id #uuid "340476c0-3dee-5eb1-8437-7f87dbcfefc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080905} {:answer/id #uuid "54f0cf44-b1e4-59c7-b3ef-da63b17de2bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080906} {:answer/id #uuid "7bf81ce1-15e2-5538-baaa-f0aa8efaf25f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080907} {:answer/id #uuid "7ef2c281-674b-51c8-9d51-07d14cbd5395" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080908}] :answer-field/id #uuid "fc40f562-5c35-56fd-a707-775ce6a52adf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080903}] :question/id #uuid "772d1cbc-9e35-56db-86ce-8d6e32198157" :question/math-academy-id "q-34814" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x) = \\frac{x}{3} + 7$ and $g(x) = 3x + k$. If $g$ is the inverse of $f$, then $k =$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083663 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083358 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab89a7ad-8b73-5dde-b5ed-efac2deb67e1" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Inverse Function Using a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ef00fae9-ff26-50ae-a63b-811be9ebb985" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080910} {:answer/id #uuid "fc3f4b18-0243-5d38-83b4-b44f4cd7969d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080911} {:answer/id #uuid "12b326bb-1fbe-543e-be66-0f2eaf3cdbda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080912} {:answer/id #uuid "69fc442c-c8b6-5a89-96a5-f1285ae1353b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080913} {:answer/id #uuid "72f42b57-c7af-5f4a-bec6-16044f7519c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080914}] :answer-field/id #uuid "8ad2c8dd-ece8-5a29-9f53-524e39065636" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080909}] :question/id #uuid "24c66bbe-f404-5177-99a4-e2606b93a2fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-34879" :question/problem "![](images/c9/c9360c7c07ecc0d78bc57fbcb56cc5ca3b799109ee48747bd4f651ad1209cd63.png)\n\nThe graph of $y = f(x)$ is given above. The points $A, B, C$, and $D$ lie on the graph, as shown. What is the value of $f^{-1}(1)$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083878 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083356 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b2b6ea7-ea1c-5d6b-b786-bb0880c6af4a" :knowledge-p